{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>The goat of the sin-offering</b> — This is the goat of the additional offerings of the new moon, and three goats were offered on that day: \"Take a goat for a sin-offering\" (Leviticus 9:3), and the goat of Nahshon (Numbers 7:16), and the goat of the new moon (ibid. 28:15). And of all of them, none was burned except this one. And the Sages of Israel disagreed about the matter: some say it was burned because of a corpse-impurity that came upon it, and some say it was burned because of אנינות (the status of a mourner before burial). This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 10:16). And according to the opinion of the one who says it was burned because of impurity, the Rav wrote on the verse \"and I ate the sin-offering today\" (Leviticus 10:19): \"today\" it is forbidden, but אנינות at night is permitted, for the status of an אונן applies only on the day of burial. For according to this, if it had been because of אנינות they should have eaten it in the evening; rather, he holds that אנינות at night is a Torah obligation."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "In the final sentence, the subject who holds אנינות at night is Torah law is understood from context to be Moses (based on Rashi's reading of the exchange), though Ramban does not name him explicitly here."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:52:20.963382683Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}